p.src:
Differentiate the sandwich with the product rule: $\frac{d}{dt}\expect{L}
=
\left(\frac{d}{dt}\bra{\psi}\right)L\ket{\psi}
+
\bra{\psi}L\left(\frac{d}{dt}\ket{\psi}\right).$ In $\hbar=1$ units, the ket equation and its Hermitian conjugate are $\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dt}\ket{\psi}&=-iH\ket{\psi},\\
\frac{d}{dt}\bra{\psi}&=+i\bra{\psi}H.\end{aligned}$ The sign of $i$ changes under complex conjugation, and $H^\dagger=H$. Substitution into Equation  gives $\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dt}\expect{L}
&=i\bra{\psi}HL\ket{\psi}
-i\bra{\psi}LH\ket{\psi}\\
&=i\bra{\psi}(HL-LH)\ket{\psi}.\end{aligned}$

