p.src:
In classical Hamiltonian mechanics, a phase-space function $L(q,p)$ with no explicit time dependence obeys $\frac{dL}{dt}=\{L,H\}_{\mathrm{PB}},$ where $\{\cdot,\cdot\}_{\mathrm{PB}}$ is the Poisson bracket. Compare this with Equation . Both brackets are antisymmetric: $\begin{aligned}
\{L,H\}_{\mathrm{PB}}&=-\{H,L\}_{\mathrm{PB}},\\
[L,H]&=-[H,L].\end{aligned}$ Both also act as derivatives on a product: $\begin{aligned}
\{LM,H\}_{\mathrm{PB}}
&=\{L,H\}_{\mathrm{PB}}M+L\{M,H\}_{\mathrm{PB}},\\
[LM,H]
&=L[M,H]+[L,H]M.
\end{aligned}$ The commutator identity follows by adding and subtracting $LHM$: $\begin{aligned}
LMH-HLM
&=(LMH-LHM)\\
&\quad +(LHM-HLM)\\
&=L(MH-HM)\\
&\quad +(LH-HL)M.\end{aligned}$

